.TH TRUENAS\-DISCOVERYD 8 "2026-04-17" "truenas-pydiscovery" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
truenas\-discoveryd \- TrueNAS unified network discovery daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B truenas\-discoveryd
.RB [ \-c
.IR CONFIG ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B truenas\-discoveryd
is a single process that hosts three network discovery protocols
in one Python event loop:
.TP
\(bu
mDNS / DNS\-SD (RFC\ 6762, RFC\ 6763)
.TP
\(bu
NetBIOS Name Service and Browser (RFC\ 1001, RFC\ 1002, MS\-BRWS)
.TP
\(bu
WS\-Discovery 1.1 (OASIS) with Microsoft PBSD extensions
.PP
Each protocol is independently supervised: a failure in one does not
bring down the others.  Each can be individually disabled via its
section's
.B enabled = false
flag in the configuration file.
.SH INTERFACE CHANGES
The daemon follows the kernel's interface and address notifications
(netlink), so no reload is needed when an interface or address comes or
goes.
Once the notifications have been quiet for one second, and at most five
seconds after the first of them, the interfaces and their addresses are
read again.
If nothing changed, as when the kernel repeats an IPv6 address on each
router advertisement, nothing happens.
Otherwise each protocol updates the interfaces whose addresses changed
and leaves the others alone:
.TP
\(bu
NetBIOS NS registers its names on the subnet of each new address and
closes the subnet of an address that is gone without releasing its
names, as Samba's nmbd does; its other subnets keep their names.
If the address it sends from on that interface goes away, it sets the
interface's remaining subnets up afresh and registers their names again.
.TP
\(bu
mDNS sends goodbyes for the address records it no longer publishes
(those of addresses that went away, and those of link\-local IPv6
addresses once the interface has one that is not link\-local),
publishes the
addresses the interface has now, and probes and announces its services
there again.
.TP
\(bu
WS\-Discovery serves the interface's new addresses and sends a Hello.
.PP
An interface listed in the configuration that does not exist yet, or
has no address yet, is served once it has one.
When a link comes back up, mDNS probes and announces on it again
(RFC\ 6762 \(sc8.3).
Updates never run alongside a reload: one requested during a reload, or
during startup, runs once that has finished, and the other way round.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " CONFIG" "\fR,\fP \-\-config " CONFIG
Path to configuration file.
Default: \fI/etc/truenas\-discovery/truenas\-discoveryd.conf\fP.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase log verbosity.
Use once for INFO, twice for DEBUG.
Without this flag, logging goes to syslog.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload configuration across every enabled protocol.
Each protocol re\-resolves interfaces, reloads local state, and
re\-announces.
.IP
Reloads never overlap.
A SIGHUP that arrives while the protocols are starting, or while a
reload is running, is held; one reload runs once that has finished,
however many signals were held.
.IP
Under systemd the unit is \fBType=notify\-reload\fP.
The daemon reports readiness (\fBREADY=1\fP) as soon as its signal
handlers are installed, before the protocols probe and register their
names, so the start timeout does not cover that work.
Startup ends with one reload pass, which applies configuration written
before readiness was reported: systemd does not signal a reload
requested before then.
Each reload is reported with \fBRELOADING=1\fP and \fBREADY=1\fP, so
\fBsystemctl reload\fP returns once the reload has finished.
systemd applies the unit's start timeout (\fBTimeoutStartSec=300\fP) to
each reload as well, including the wait for startup to finish when the
reload was requested during startup.
.IP
The mDNS host name is an exception: it is established by probing at
startup and is not re\-read on reload.  Once running, it changes only
through RFC 6762 conflict resolution, when another host on the link
claims the same name and this one renames itself (for example
\fBnas.local\fP to \fBnas\-2.local\fP).  Re\-deriving the name from
the configuration file on every reload would silently undo that and
put the daemon back onto the contested name.  To change the
advertised host name, restart the service rather than reloading it;
the name won by conflict resolution is not persisted, so a restart
begins again from the configured name.  The current name is reported
as \fBhostname\fP in the mDNS \fBstatus.json\fP.
If every rename keeps colliding, the daemon stops retrying after 20
attempts and logs an error; its address records stay withdrawn until
a reload that changes interfaces or protocols, or a restart, begins
probing afresh.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Write runtime status for every enabled protocol to its per\-protocol
status.json under \fI/run/truenas\-discovery\fP.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Graceful shutdown: mDNS sends goodbyes, WS\-Discovery sends a Bye, and
NetBIOS NS sends a host announcement marking the server removed, then
each closes its sockets.
Like Samba's nmbd, NetBIOS NS releases no names at shutdown.
A shutdown requested during startup abandons the rest of startup, one
requested during a reload abandons the reload, and a held SIGHUP is
dropped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/truenas\-discovery/truenas\-discoveryd.conf
Unified daemon configuration (INI format).
.TP
.I /etc/truenas\-discovery/services.d/*.conf
mDNS service definition files (one per service).
.TP
.I /run/truenas\-discovery/mdns/status.json
mDNS runtime status (SIGUSR1).
.TP
.I /run/truenas\-discovery/netbiosns/status.json
NetBIOS NS runtime status.
.TP
.I /run/truenas\-discovery/wsd/status.json
WS\-Discovery runtime status.
